    "case_id": "709",
    "case_summary": "A 65-year old male presented with persistent high-grade fever, nausea, vomiting and generalized weakness for 10 days. Prior history included one month of intermittent high-grade fever, vomiting, loss of weight and night-sweats. Medical history significant for hypertension (6 months) and benign prostate hyperplasia (2 years) with regular medication.\n\nPatient was conscious, well-built and oriented but lethargic. Temperature: 101°F. Physical findings included mild hepatosplenomegaly and conjunctival pallor.\n\n- Laboratory Tests:    * Complete Blood Count: Hemoglobin 9.4 g/dL, leukocyte count 2.94 × 103/μL, platelet count 90 × 103/μL, MCV 78 fL    * Chemistry: Elevated liver transaminases (>3x normal), total bilirubin 0.85 mg/dL, LDH 1552.30 IU/L, ferritin >1700ng/mL, hyponatremia (117 mmol/L), total protein 4.72 g/dL    * Serology: Negative for hepatitis B/C, HIV, malaria, dengue, and scrub typhus    * Culture: Negative blood, urine, and sputum cultures- Imaging Studies:    * Radiological evaluation showed hepatosplenomegaly with fatty liver, mild urinary bladder wall thickening    * CECT thorax and abdomen: Mild hepatomegaly and prostatomegaly    * PET Scan: Hepatomegaly with external iliac lymphadenopathy- Bone Marrow Studies:    Title: Bone marrow and trephine biopsy    Description: Erythroid hyperplasia with megaloblastic erythropoiesis and evidence of erythroid and lymphophagocytosis    - Molecular Testing:    TrueNat MTB/RIF Assay: MTB detected with Rifampicin indeterminate",
